<node id="S:1.1">
  <data key="d31">Software engineering begins before the code</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C01</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">1.1</data>
  <data key="d51">Begin with established evidence on how professional developers spend time understanding, modifying, testing and integrating systems. Distinguish typing a patch from delivering an accepted, operable change, then introduce agentic-software-engineering visions explicitly as proposed extensions rather than as measured proof.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:1.2">
  <data key="d31">From autocomplete to delegated execution</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C01</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">1.2</data>
  <data key="d51">Introduce completion, conversational assistance, repository-aware agents and multi-step autonomous execution as progressively larger delegations. Explain which choices remain human-owned at each level and why increased autonomy changes coordination and control costs.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:1.3">
  <data key="d31">The engineer's work before and after AI</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C01</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">1.3</data>
  <data key="d51">Describe the transfer of effort from typing and routine lookup toward problem framing, architecture, checking, integration and responsibility. Treat changes in developer activity as hypotheses to test, not as universal workforce predictions.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:1.4">
  <data key="d31">The lifecycle of a trustworthy change</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C01</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">1.4</data>
  <data key="d51">Trace stakeholder intention, discovery, design options, implementation, independently specified checks, human review, deployment and feedback. Define the change dossier and responsible decision-maker at each handoff. This is the author's engineering reference workflow, motivated by software lifecycle evidence and agent-governance proposals rather than experimentally validated as a whole.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:1.5">
  <data key="d31">The autonomy–accountability boundary</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C01</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">1.5</data>
  <data key="d51">Separate permission to act from authority to approve residual risk. Discuss human-on-the-loop and human-in-the-loop as design choices conditioned by impact and observability rather than slogans.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:1.6">
  <data key="d31">Why brownfield change starts with system comprehension</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C01</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">1.6</data>
  <data key="d51">Introduce the existing system's implicit invariants, distributed rationale and human ownership as prerequisites to a safe code edit. An early minimum context package must exist before Chapter 3 lets an agent inspect the repository; Chapter 9 will later explain how to preserve and update that knowledge durably.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:1.7">
  <data key="d31">What the research can and cannot tell us</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C01</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">1.7</data>
  <data key="d51">Explain study types before citing effects: controlled experiments, repository observation, surveys, benchmarks, industrial reports and position papers. Distinguish observed measurements from recommended governance processes, preparing readers to interpret the evidence critically.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:1.8">
  <data key="d31">A teaching case, not a reported field experiment</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C01</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">1.8</data>
  <data key="d51">Introduce the invented transaction-approval API, its legacy interfaces, ambiguous regional threshold request and audit obligations. Make the system boundary and synthetic data explicit. Ownership and review findings explain why the example is realistic; no cited paper is claimed to have studied this particular service.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:1.9">
  <data key="d31">From generated output to an evidence question</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C01</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">1.9</data>
  <data key="d51">Summarize why the reader must ask what changed, how it was checked and what consequence followed. Define the initial questions to be answered in a controlled pilot and bridge to a rigorous discussion of productivity.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:2.1">
  <data key="d31">The productivity denominator problem</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C02</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">2.1</data>
  <data key="d51">Explain why suggestions accepted, tokens generated, commits, pull requests, merged changes, releases and user outcomes form different denominators. Construct a funnel in which gains can disappear at review or integration.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:2.2">
  <data key="d31">How to read causal evidence</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C02</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">2.2</data>
  <data key="d51">Teach random assignment, matched comparison, before/after measurements, selection effects and confidence intervals using developer-tool experiments. State what a reported percentage does and does not quantify.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:2.3">
  <data key="d31">Why controlled studies disagree</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C02</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">2.3</data>
  <data key="d51">Compare positive enterprise evidence with the slower results for experienced open-source maintainers, making task familiarity, tool era, environment and developer cohort explicit. The object is conditional understanding, not a synthesized universal multiplier.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:2.4">
  <data key="d31">Observational adoption and its confounders</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C02</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">2.4</data>
  <data key="d51">Examine repository adoption studies and agent trace analyses. Discuss parallel trend assumptions, tooling changes, self-selection, repository maturity and the limits of activity telemetry.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:2.5">
  <data key="d31">Writing code versus shipping code</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C02</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">2.5</data>
  <data key="d51">Introduce work in progress, review capacity, testing and release mechanisms as bottlenecks. Build a simple throughput model that explains why significantly more code and commits need not produce proportionately more deployed value.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:2.6">
  <data key="d31">Quality-adjusted productivity</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C02</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">2.6</data>
  <data key="d51">Treat complexity, post-merge rework, reviewer effort, rollback and incident risk as potential costs of faster output. Use longitudinal and maintenance studies as narrower empirical signals; position commercial industry reports as descriptive comparisons rather than causal estimates.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:2.7">
  <data key="d31">Developer experience and hidden time</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C02</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">2.7</data>
  <data key="d51">Compare measured task time with perceived savings, context switching, flow and debugging effort. Explain why surveys and tool dashboards are informative about adoption but insufficient for causal conclusions.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:2.8">
  <data key="d31">Designing a coding-specific field pilot</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C02</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">2.8</data>
  <data key="d51">Set up a preregistered experiment on comparable software-maintenance tasks with a fixed observation period, quality guardrails, reviewer-minute accounting, random assignment where feasible, and documented statistical power. Enterprise coding RCTs are direct precedents; an identity-administration RCT is useful only as a cross-domain illustration of experiment design.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:2.9">
  <data key="d31">From metrics to an evidence-based pilot decision</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C02</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">2.9</data>
  <data key="d51">Combine measures of delivery, change failure, security, review load, human time and learning into a predeclared pilot scorecard, with uncertainty and stop criteria. DORA's ROI guide supplies practitioner calculation structure, not a new controlled-effect estimate; governance design is the author's proposed operational response.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:3.1">
  <data key="d31">From suggestion to a permission-bounded agent</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C03</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">3.1</data>
  <data key="d51">Describe repository reads, plans, shell tools, patches, test execution and stateful observations, but establish workspace containment, synthetic fixtures, restricted shell and no production credentials *before* the first agent action. This minimal safe runtime is elaborated into a full threat model in Chapter 6.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:3.2">
  <data key="d31">What an agent actually observes</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C03</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">3.2</data>
  <data key="d51">Distinguish source files, build outputs, test results, tool responses, retrieval context and missing organizational knowledge. Show how partial observation yields confident but incorrect modifications.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:3.3">
  <data key="d31">The task envelope</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C03</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">3.3</data>
  <data key="d51">Define task size, blast radius, dependency coupling, domain novelty and availability of external checks. Propose routing rules for completion, supervised agent work or full human ownership rather than scaling autonomy blindly.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:3.4">
  <data key="d31">Agent benchmarks and observed pull requests</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C03</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">3.4</data>
  <data key="d51">Separate evaluation on constructed or selected tasks from naturally occurring GitHub agent PRs and from spectacular long-horizon demonstrations. Check task selection, contamination, pass criteria, human intervention and data lineage before translating any reported success into production reliability; MirrorCode is capability evidence, not a proven autonomous calendar-time SLA.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:3.5">
  <data key="d31">Common failure modes in real changes</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C03</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">3.5</data>
  <data key="d51">Organize failures into misunderstood requirements, inadequate context, API mismatch, build or dependency problems, speculative fixes and integration friction. Show why retries without a diagnosis can repeat the same error.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:3.6">
  <data key="d31">A bounded propose–act–observe loop</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C03</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">3.6</data>
  <data key="d51">Build an explicit task state machine: intake, plan, limited tool actions, observation, independent check, budget exhaustion, handoff. Production coding traces and failed agent PRs motivate failure handling; deterministic orchestration results from non-SWE tool workflows are architectural analogies, not proof that this loop will succeed on brownfield tasks.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:3.7">
  <data key="d31">When parallel agents help and hurt</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C03</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">3.7</data>
  <data key="d51">Explain decomposition of independent work, merge conflicts, overlapping context assumptions and correlated failures. Separate the theoretical appeal of parallelism from evidence about actual project throughput.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:3.8">
  <data key="d31">Stopping rules versus acceptance authority</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C03</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">3.8</data>
  <data key="d51">Explain local stopping, retry budgets, external failure signals and human escalation as an implementable policy. A passing test or a self-review earns readiness for independent review, not permission to deploy; specify a bounded fallback when two orchestrator rejections cannot cite a policy reason.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:3.9">
  <data key="d31">Agent capability as a testable hypothesis</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C03</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">3.9</data>
  <data key="d51">Close by specifying an observable candidate output, evidence required for acceptance and the human who owns remaining uncertainty. The gap between prompt and approved intent motivates specification-driven engineering.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:4.1">
  <data key="d31">Separate intention determination from design</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C04</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">4.1</data>
  <data key="d51">Introduce problem framing, stakeholder analysis, behavioral boundaries and unacceptable outcomes before asking for a solution. Explain how premature code generation anchors the team to an unvalidated interpretation of the request.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:4.2">
  <data key="d31">From a request to a versioned intention ledger</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C04</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">4.2</data>
  <data key="d51">Show a lightweight ledger with stakeholder, desired outcome, assumptions, open questions, uncertainty, prohibitions and authoritative sign-off. Treat this ledger as the book's proposed agile practice; studies of OSS governance and agentic engineering motivate traceability, but do not validate this specific artifact.</data>
  <data key="d52">engineering proposal</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:4.3">
  <data key="d31">Write behavioral examples before implementation</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C04</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">4.3</data>
  <data key="d51">Turn stakeholder-approved outcomes into concrete examples, invariants, negative cases and backwards-compatibility constraints. Distinguish an independently confirmed rule from a specification docstring inferred by an LLM from code: the latter reduces some test misguidance in Zhao et al. but cannot establish that the inferred rule matches stakeholder intent.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:4.4">
  <data key="d31">Executable contracts without circular oracles</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C04</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">4.4</data>
  <data key="d51">Map authoritative examples to deterministic assertions, schemas, properties and negative tests. Explain the self-confirmation failure when generated tests copy faulty implementation behavior, and the limited mitigation reported by specification-based prompting. The authoritative acceptance oracle must be reviewed independently from the produced patch.</data>
  <data key="d52">engineering proposal</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:4.5">
  <data key="d31">Architecture decision versus acceptance contract</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C04</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">4.5</data>
  <data key="d51">Teach readers to keep the fixed intent separate from several candidate implementation designs. Design constraints can be negotiated, but changes to user-visible semantics require renewed authorization.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:4.6">
  <data key="d31">A delta-driven iterative brownfield lifecycle</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C04</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">4.6</data>
  <data key="d51">Propose short cycles: examine the existing behavior, clarify and version the next intention delta, analyze design impact, implement one reversible slice, independently test, review, deploy and learn. Repository studies motivate the need for bounded changes, while FlowGen compares simulated Scrum/TDD/Waterfall processes on coding benchmarks—not real agile brownfield team outcomes.</data>
  <data key="d52">engineering proposal</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:4.7">
  <data key="d31">Late intentions and explicit impact analysis</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C04</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">4.7</data>
  <data key="d51">When a new regional requirement arrives after code exists, retain the previous intent revision; record who authorized the change, which design alternatives and checks it invalidates, and whether to amend, roll back or defer the implementation. Code-critique and OSS-policy observations motivate traceability; the delta/impact workflow is a proposed method rather than a measured benefit of those studies.</data>
  <data key="d52">engineering proposal</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:4.8">
  <data key="d31">Native intent gates and optional compatibility adapters</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C04</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">4.8</data>
  <data key="d51">Define native gates on approved intention, invariants, architecture decisions, verification evidence and acceptance authority. Keep a legacy user-story/test-case view as an optional adapter when integrating established CI, rather than treating it as canonical. Use the CCS profile as a worked engineering example, not a method empirically demonstrated by Farrag or Qiu.</data>
  <data key="d52">engineering proposal</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:4.9">
  <data key="d31">The approval-rule contract across two iterations</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C04</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">4.9</data>
  <data key="d51">Show a complete before/after trace of the fictional approval service: original ambiguity, an agreed acceptance contract, a first implementation delta, a late regional exception, the new contract revision and the test diff. Explicitly mark which artifacts are invented pedagogical examples and which limitations are informed by empirical work on intent drift and review.</data>
  <data key="d52">engineering proposal</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:5.1">
  <data key="d31">What counts as code quality?</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C05</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">5.1</data>
  <data key="d51">Establish correctness, readability, cohesion, coupling, duplication, complexity, changeability and operational reliability as separate dimensions. Explain why an all-purpose quality score obscures important trade-offs.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:5.2">
  <data key="d31">Reading AI-versus-human studies correctly</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C05</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">5.2</data>
  <data key="d51">Discuss code-unit selection, languages, code size, authorship inference, matched baselines and static-analysis definitions. Use conflicting results to demonstrate why no universal AI-code superiority or inferiority claim is warranted.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:5.3">
  <data key="d31">Code smells are signals, not incidents</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C05</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">5.3</data>
  <data key="d51">Separate static warnings, complexity growth and duplication from externally observed faults and post-merge maintenance. The Cursor longitudinal study and real-world technical-debt observations support narrower outcomes; commit-volume studies offer an *activity* comparison rather than direct evidence of architectural smells.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:5.4">
  <data key="d31">Generated duplication and missed reuse</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C05</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">5.4</data>
  <data key="d51">Examine why agents may create a new helper instead of reusing established abstractions, or reproduce code patterns that appear appropriate locally. Introduce repository-aware design checks and dependency search before editing.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:5.5">
  <data key="d31">Architectural fit in a living codebase</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C05</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">5.5</data>
  <data key="d51">Evaluate stable module boundaries, reuse, data ownership, error handling and compatibility for the running change. Contrast a narrowly passing test with good architectural placement; the architecture-smell and code-reuse studies give direct signals, while historical code ownership data inform stewardship rather than prove a particular refactoring is correct.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:5.6">
  <data key="d31">Post-merge evolution and maintenance burden</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C05</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">5.6</data>
  <data key="d51">Assess follow-up fixes, code smells, reverts and reviewer corrections after merge. Explain how task type, PR size and agent identity interact with observed quality metrics.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:5.7">
  <data key="d31">Tracing quality across agents and projects</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C05</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">5.7</data>
  <data key="d51">Compare code emitted by different agents only after normalizing task complexity and project context. Distinguish acceptance and maintenance outcomes from intrinsic model capability.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:5.8">
  <data key="d31">Authorship is not stewardship</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C05</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">5.8</data>
  <data key="d51">Distinguish generated-code provenance from responsibility to explain, maintain and recover a component. Historic ownership–quality studies provide measured background; the claim that AI invalidates traditional authorship metrics is a reasoned proposal that deserves empirical testing. Do not use newcomer-participation statistics as proof of the same effect.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:5.9">
  <data key="d31">Architecture fitness in the approval service</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C05</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">5.9</data>
  <data key="d51">Compare the duplicated-rule patch and the domain-policy-module alternative against checks for stable public APIs, invariants, error semantics, expected reuse and rollback cost. Explain the rationale for choosing one design; these are proposed engineering fitness criteria inferred from smell and reuse studies, not metrics with a universal safe threshold.</data>
  <data key="d52">engineering proposal</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:6.1">
  <data key="d31">Two attack surfaces, two threat models</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C06</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">6.1</data>
  <data key="d51">Distinguish vulnerabilities introduced into product code from attacks through the editor, shell, tool adapter or agent harness. Identify assets, trust boundaries, attacker-controlled text and possible effects.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:6.2">
  <data key="d31">Functional correctness is not security</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C06</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">6.2</data>
  <data key="d51">Demonstrate how a transaction approval service may function under normal inputs yet leak data, bypass authorization or permit a malformed operation. Make separate correctness and secure-behavior criteria explicit.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:6.3">
  <data key="d31">What vulnerability studies actually measure</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C06</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">6.3</data>
  <data key="d51">Explain benchmark prompts, scanner detections, repository selection, language differences and matched-human controls. Avoid extrapolating a vulnerability percentage beyond the study's method and population.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:6.4">
  <data key="d31">Security-directed generation and remediation</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C06</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">6.4</data>
  <data key="d51">Compare unstructured requests for secure code against concrete feedback from scanners, taint analysis and vulnerability examples. Show why external localized findings can be more reliable than asking a model to reassure itself.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:6.5">
  <data key="d31">Dependency provenance and hallucinated packages</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C06</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">6.5</data>
  <data key="d51">Trace an assistant-recommended library from generated import to registry resolution, signature or source integrity, allowed versions and transitive dependencies. Distinguish generating a nonexistent name from an actual compromised dependency.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:6.6">
  <data key="d31">Prompt injection through repository and tools</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C06</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">6.6</data>
  <data key="d51">Show how README files, comments, issue text and tool output can smuggle instructions into an agent action chain. Teach the separation of untrusted data from authority-bearing instructions and enforce execution controls outside the model.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:6.7">
  <data key="d31">Harness configuration, skills and external services</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C06</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">6.7</data>
  <data key="d51">Inspect approval scopes, shell access, MCP servers, extension manifests, logging, network access and secret injection. Define a configuration baseline with least privilege and explicitly trusted tool identities.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:6.8">
  <data key="d31">A secure agent execution profile</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C06</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">6.8</data>
  <data key="d51">Construct a sandboxed run with short-lived credentials, network restrictions, pinned tools, audit logs and explicit human grants for destructive operations. Present these as defensible engineering controls whose effectiveness still requires testing.</data>
  <data key="d52">engineering proposal</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:6.9">
  <data key="d31">Security evidence and release decision</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C06</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">6.9</data>
  <data key="d51">Connect threat model, relevant tests, findings, accepted exceptions and provenance to the change dossier. Require an accountable owner to accept residual security risk instead of allowing agent confidence or functional tests to do so.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:7.1">
  <data key="d31">The oracle problem in AI-generated software</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C07</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">7.1</data>
  <data key="d51">Define correctness claims, expected outcomes and the difference between independent requirements and implementation-derived assumptions. Explain why more tests can improve confidence only if they challenge the right behavior.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:7.2">
  <data key="d31">A hierarchy of evidence</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C07</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">7.2</data>
  <data key="d51">Arrange static checks, unit tests, contract tests, integration tests, security tests, property checks, end-to-end tests and production monitoring by what each can actually prove. Resist the temptation to equate passing tests with absence of defects.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:7.3">
  <data key="d31">Preventing bug-shaped test generation</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C07</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">7.3</data>
  <data key="d51">Explain how a model writing tests against buggy code may encode the implementation's mistake as the expected result. Require tests to originate from independent examples or invariants wherever possible.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:7.4">
  <data key="d31">Regression tests for existing and changed behavior</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C07</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">7.4</data>
  <data key="d51">Derive old/new behavior tests from explicit domain partitions, current production contracts, boundary cases, invalid input and concurrency. Contrast with agent-written tests whose expected values can be biased by buggy code; FlowGen coding-benchmark results are not evidence about real-world brownfield regression-suite effectiveness.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:7.5">
  <data key="d31">Properties and adversarial counterexamples</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C07</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">7.5</data>
  <data key="d51">Formulate invariants such as 'an unapproved transaction cannot be committed' or 'each approval yields one audit event'. Add property-based generation and fault injection, defining the test objective before prompting the agent.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:7.6">
  <data key="d31">Benchmark success versus merge readiness</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C07</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">7.6</data>
  <data key="d51">Compare benchmark scores, human-maintainer criteria and realistic integration tasks. A patch that passes the designated test set may still be unacceptable due to maintainability, unintended changes or repository-specific constraints.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:7.7">
  <data key="d31">Independent checkers and correlated error</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C07</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">7.7</data>
  <data key="d51">Compare deterministic analyzers, human checkers, and a distinct LLM reviewing generated code. Use software-focused cross-model review results together with broader correlated-error research, being careful that model diversity is not statistical independence and that non-code judge benchmarks do not directly quantify SWE defect rates.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:7.8">
  <data key="d31">Bounded repair versus independent acceptance</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C07</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">7.8</data>
  <data key="d51">Specify a bounded loop of diagnose, attempt, retest and evidence collection, followed by an external checker and human owner. A local pass is only review-ready; repeated rejection without a cited policy or changed evidence triggers adjudication instead of unbounded reruns. The exact budget and escalation policy is an author-proposed mechanism to pilot.</data>
  <data key="d52">engineering proposal</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:7.9">
  <data key="d31">The acceptance evidence dossier</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C07</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">7.9</data>
  <data key="d51">Produce a compact change dossier containing intent version, design record, exact diff, test provenance, security results, residual risks and open questions. Distinguish 'ready for review' from 'accepted for deployment'.</data>
  <data key="d52">engineering proposal</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:8.1">
  <data key="d31">Why review becomes the scarce resource</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C08</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">8.1</data>
  <data key="d51">Show how faster generation can increase PR arrival rate, review queues, maintainer load and unmerged work. Connect review economics to the earlier distinction between code production and delivered value.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:8.2">
  <data key="d31">The influence of code complexity and provenance</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C08</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">8.2</data>
  <data key="d51">Examine controlled evidence on reviewer attention, code complexity and the AI label. Explain why spending more time scrutinizing AI code does not automatically imply better defect detection.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:8.3">
  <data key="d31">Human intervention in agent pull requests</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C08</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">8.3</data>
  <data key="d51">Describe where maintainers revise, guide, approve or reject agent-authored changes, noting task selection and PR size. Use real PR traces as descriptive evidence of workflow, not universal correctness rates.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:8.4">
  <data key="d31">What AI reviewers catch—and miss</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C08</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">8.4</data>
  <data key="d51">Compare field experience of automated review tools with experimental evaluation of functional and architectural defect detection. Separate faster cycle time and fewer comments from the quality of the merged changes.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:8.5">
  <data key="d31">False positives, verbosity and developer response</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C08</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">8.5</data>
  <data key="d51">Explain why irrelevant comments impose reading cost and reduce confidence. Design review output as short, testable claims tied to concrete lines, failures or intent deltas.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:8.6">
  <data key="d31">Self-review, cross-model review and shared blind spots</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C08</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">8.6</data>
  <data key="d51">Explore model self-preference, correlated errors, different reviewer model orderings and independent critical checks. Show when extra critics help and when they merely repeat assumptions.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:8.7">
  <data key="d31">Automation bias in software review</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C08</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">8.7</data>
  <data key="d51">Anchor trust calibration in code-review provenance and gaze studies; add the broader automation-bias literature as a boundary condition rather than a directly transferable measured rate. Teach seeded-defect reviewer exercises and explicit reasons to override AI recommendations, while marking their benefit as an evaluation hypothesis.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:8.8">
  <data key="d31">Risk-based review allocation</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C08</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">8.8</data>
  <data key="d51">Route trivial, reversible changes through high automation and high-impact changes through explicit human review. Define risk features such as touched subsystem, permission changes, behavioral novelty, uncertainty and blast radius.</data>
  <data key="d52">engineering proposal</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:8.9">
  <data key="d31">From comments to accountable acceptance</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C08</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">8.9</data>
  <data key="d51">Establish a review protocol: check intent adherence, challenge evidence, identify unexplained behavior, decide on fixes or escalation, and record who accepts remaining risk. Close the review by updating shared system knowledge.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:9.1">
  <data key="d31">Why comprehension dominates brownfield work</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C09</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">9.1</data>
  <data key="d51">Start from classic field studies of navigation, code reading and maintenance to explain how much of development involves reconstructing existing behavior. Use this foundation to interpret the promises and limits of retrieval assistants.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:9.2">
  <data key="d31">Mental models, rationale and cognitive load</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C09</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">9.2</data>
  <data key="d51">Explain how engineers infer architecture, data flow, exceptional behavior and design intention from imperfect artifacts. Contrast source syntax with the higher-level understanding needed to make safe changes.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:9.3">
  <data key="d31">Authorship, ownership and stewardship</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C09</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">9.3</data>
  <data key="d51">Review research on ownership concentration, knowledge transfer and defect associations. Explain why AI code generation breaks the inference that the person whose account produced a commit necessarily understands it.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:9.4">
  <data key="d31">Context assembly for an agent</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C09</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">9.4</data>
  <data key="d51">Define the minimal context package for a bounded task: affected APIs, dependent modules, domain rules, prior decisions, tests and trusted references. Discuss retrieval misses, irrelevant context and source hierarchy.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:9.5">
  <data key="d31">Different forms of persistent memory</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C09</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">9.5</data>
  <data key="d51">Separate working context, episodic execution traces, durable semantic knowledge, structured entity relations and authoritative decision records. Contrast promising research mechanisms with what has actually been evaluated.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:9.6">
  <data key="d31">Memory consistency, decay and invalidation</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C09</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">9.6</data>
  <data key="d51">Describe how facts become stale as branches merge, policy decisions change and owners depart. Propose versioned memory entries with evidence links, time scope, explicit invalidation triggers and a human-owned conflict resolution path.</data>
  <data key="d52">engineering proposal</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:9.7">
  <data key="d31">A typed graph for software-specific knowledge</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C09</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">9.7</data>
  <data key="d51">Model contracts, modules, test oracles, reviewers, owners, incidents and architecture decisions as versioned, typed links. AriGraph demonstrates one possible episodic–semantic agent memory representation outside software engineering, not an empirical proof that a software knowledge graph improves maintenance outcomes; historical turnover research motivates the need.</data>
  <data key="d52">engineering proposal</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:9.8">
  <data key="d31">Turnover, succession and long-term maintenance</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C09</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">9.8</data>
  <data key="d51">Examine the historic evidence on developer departure and succession costs. Design handover records around rationale, untested assumptions, failure modes and ownership of future modifications.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:9.9">
  <data key="d31">A verified memory handoff</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C09</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">9.9</data>
  <data key="d51">Complete the running case by storing approved intent and design decisions with links to tests, diff and reviewers. Add an invalidation example when the jurisdiction rule changes, then ask a new engineer to explain the system unaided.</data>
  <data key="d52">engineering proposal</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:10.1">
  <data key="d31">Short-term task success versus retained understanding</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C10</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">10.1</data>
  <data key="d51">Distinguish task correctness during AI assistance from comprehension measured when assistance is removed. Prioritize developer learning experiments, compare beginner-code comprehension studies, and explicitly avoid transferring numeric effects from essay-writing EEG experiments to software programming.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:10.2">
  <data key="d31">Comprehension debt is distinct from technical debt</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C10</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">10.2</data>
  <data key="d51">Define a gap between code shipped and knowledge retained by the team. Ground the term in diary observations from student software projects, and contrast those qualitative patterns with controlled comprehension experiments; do not claim a quantified professional-team effect solely from student self-reports.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:10.3">
  <data key="d31">Novices, misconceptions and verification difficulty</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C10</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">10.3</data>
  <data key="d51">Show why beginners may accept code they cannot evaluate, produce changes that burden maintainers and struggle to detect violations of a natural-language request. Explain the risks without treating all novice use as harmful.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:10.4">
  <data key="d31">Cognitive offloading, physiology and limitations</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C10</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">10.4</data>
  <data key="d51">Separate the theoretical augmentation-trap hypothesis from biometric measurements made during AI-assisted coding and from essay-writing EEG results. Physiological activation is not a direct proxy for software correctness or durable learning; present testable hypotheses for the running case.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:10.5">
  <data key="d31">Learning through explanations, repairs and code tours</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C10</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">10.5</data>
  <data key="d51">Contrast model-only explanations with expert-curated tours, debugging exercises and tests built by the learner. Show how scaffolding can turn assistance into active learning instead of answer consumption.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:10.6">
  <data key="d31">Onboarding is a long process, not a first PR</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C10</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">10.6</data>
  <data key="d51">Use classic organizational studies to distinguish first edit, first independent task, file-level breadth and mastery of central components. Identify what an agent can accelerate and what requires social and architectural participation.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:10.7">
  <data key="d31">Mentoring and apprenticeship in a team with agents</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C10</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">10.7</data>
  <data key="d51">Propose explicit learning opportunities: requirements interviews, adversarial testing, reviewer rotation, post-incident explanations and teach-back sessions. Link the choices to the risk that automation removes entry-level tasks that once built expertise.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:10.8">
  <data key="d31">Measuring retention and engineering fluency</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C10</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">10.8</data>
  <data key="d51">Define assessments such as independent bug diagnosis, rationale recall, transfer to an unfamiliar module and maintenance after thirty days. Treat self-rated confidence and IDE activity as supplementary, not decisive, measures.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:10.9">
  <data key="d31">A practical capability-development contract</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C10</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">10.9</data>
  <data key="d51">End with an onboarding plan for the approval subsystem: reading map, bounded agent use, one unassisted diagnosis, a supervised review and scheduled follow-up. Connect individual competence to team-level allocation of responsibility.</data>
  <data key="d52">engineering proposal</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:11.1">
  <data key="d31">From individual productivity to team throughput</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C11</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">11.1</data>
  <data key="d51">Discuss interfaces between feature engineers, reviewers, SRE, security, product and architecture. Model queueing effects when agent output accelerates faster than human validation capacity.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:11.2">
  <data key="d31">New division of labor in engineering teams</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C11</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">11.2</data>
  <data key="d51">Distinguish intent owner, system steward, agent operator, verifier, reviewer, release authority and incident responder. Avoid treating these as necessarily new job titles; often they are explicit accountabilities within existing roles.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:11.3">
  <data key="d31">Policies that make expectations visible</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C11</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">11.3</data>
  <data key="d51">Compare permissive, restrictive and conditional open-source AI-use policies. Explain why disclosure, traceability, quality expectations and author responsibility may be more practical than blanket acceptance or blanket bans.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:11.4">
  <data key="d31">Executable policy as an engineering proposal</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C11</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">11.4</data>
  <data key="d51">Translate clear authority boundaries into checks for allowed tools, data handling, intent revision, test provenance, review thresholds and deployment permissions. Blueprint First/Model Second demonstrates constrained orchestration on a non-SWE tool benchmark; it supplies a design analogy rather than enterprise evidence for this policy stack.</data>
  <data key="d52">engineering proposal</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:11.5">
  <data key="d31">Agile team work with agent-produced increments</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C11</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">11.5</data>
  <data key="d51">Carry a late requirements change across backlog, versioned contract, independent tests and safe incremental release while several agents operate. Use observed agent-PR review and OSS policy practices to motivate coordination; explicitly resist inferring real-team agile throughput from FlowGen's code-generation benchmarks.</data>
  <data key="d52">engineering proposal</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:11.6">
  <data key="d31">Human accountability, conflicts and escalation</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C11</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">11.6</data>
  <data key="d51">Define who may authorize risky operations, interpret ambiguous requirements or accept an exception. Specify a path when the loop passes its checks but an orchestrator or reviewer disagrees, including reasons, limited retries and human arbitration.</data>
  <data key="d52">engineering proposal</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:11.7">
  <data key="d31">Maintainer load and newcomer participation</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C11</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">11.7</data>
  <data key="d51">Separate per-PR review burden, aggregate maintainer queues and newcomer participation effects rather than assuming that more complex generated changes automatically exclude newcomers. Combine qualitative slop observations with the causal newcomer-participation study, distinguishing its estimated association from a universal rule.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:11.8">
  <data key="d31">Knowledge handoff and stewardship rights</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C11</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">11.8</data>
  <data key="d51">Make ownership transfer operational: name current system stewards, define who can modify acceptance contracts, schedule knowledge refresh and attach verified design rationale to handover. This applies the turnover and succession evidence of Chapter 9 to a team policy decision, rather than repeating a literature review of code ownership.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:11.9">
  <data key="d31">Staged deployment gates, not a guaranteed ROI</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C11</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">11.9</data>
  <data key="d51">Build a rollout policy spanning sandbox pilots, low-risk changes and audited production use, with limits on review queues and rollback thresholds. DORA ROI is a practitioner planning framework and vendor/industry reports are contextual, not proof that a particular deployment stage yields a given return.</data>
  <data key="d52">engineering proposal</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:12.1">
  <data key="d31">Economics of the entire engineering loop</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C12</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">12.1</data>
  <data key="d51">Define the total cost of an accepted change: discovery, development, verification, review, tool consumption, infrastructure, integration, operations, remediation and learning. Distinguish cash costs, capacity costs and risk-weighted losses.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:12.2">
  <data key="d31">Why cheap generation may increase total cost</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C12</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">12.2</data>
  <data key="d51">Connect increased code and commit activity to reviewer queues, larger changes, incidents, complexity and delayed maintenance. Show the conditions under which local speed gains disappear from long-run economics.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:12.3">
  <data key="d31">Choosing the least-cost adequate level of autonomy</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C12</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">12.3</data>
  <data key="d51">Present a decision model comparing human-only, pair assistance, bounded agent and agent-plus-independent-checker. Define acceptance probabilities, verifier effort and uncertain loss as parameters to measure in a local pilot, not as empirically known constants obtained from conceptual position papers.</data>
  <data key="d52">engineering proposal</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:12.4">
  <data key="d31">Estimating return without fabricated certainty</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C12</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">12.4</data>
  <data key="d51">Teach incremental ROI, baselines, opportunity cost, treatment spillovers, observation periods and uncertainty intervals. Reserve scenario estimates for decision analysis and do not present assumed review costs as measured empirical facts.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:12.5">
  <data key="d31">Jobs, hiring, vacancy flows and unequal cohorts</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C12</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">12.5</data>
  <data key="d51">Keep employment stocks, hiring, vacancies, promotions and job postings distinct. Compare the version-specific Stanford early-career estimate, firm-level Copilot hiring evidence, and Norwegian administrative findings as outcomes drawn from different settings—not independent replications of the same causal effect.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:12.6">
  <data key="d31">Alternative causes of junior-market change</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C12</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">12.6</data>
  <data key="d51">Compare long-run vacancy timing, seniority-skewed job postings, remote mentorship and macroeconomic explanations before attributing junior outcomes to coding agents. Gallacher's Indeed article is descriptive trend analysis, *not* a randomized experiment, and chronology alone does not establish causality.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:12.7">
  <data key="d31">The apprenticeship and long-term skill economy</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C12</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">12.7</data>
  <data key="d51">Connect organizational demand for experienced engineers with the risk that entry-level work is automated away. Model the future cost of losing training pathways rather than equating fewer junior tasks with efficiency.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:12.8">
  <data key="d31">Distributional effects and quality of forecasts</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C12</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">12.8</data>
  <data key="d51">Compare country studies, firm-level spending/hiring outcomes, skeptical economy-wide assessments and executive expectations without equating their endpoints or controls. Separate observed employment patterns from modeled long-run responses and survey expectations; headline national trends cannot identify an AI-specific effect by themselves.</data>
  <data key="d52">research discussion</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
<node id="S:12.9">
  <data key="d31">A reversible, risk-adjusted adoption decision</data>
  <data key="d32">section</data>
  <data key="d33">C12</data>
  <data key="d34"></data>
  <data key="d35"></data>
  <data key="d36"></data>
  <data key="d37"></data>
  <data key="d38"></data>
  <data key="d39"></data>
  <data key="d40"></data>
  <data key="d41"></data>
  <data key="d42"></data>
  <data key="d43"></data>
  <data key="d44"></data>
  <data key="d45"></data>
  <data key="d46"></data>
  <data key="d47"></data>
  <data key="d48"></data>
  <data key="d49"></data>
  <data key="d50">12.9</data>
  <data key="d51">Integrate measured cost per accepted change, quality, security, review capacity, learning and operational risk into a decision memo with uncertainty bounds, named owners and a rollback plan. DORA supplies practitioner ROI structure and governance papers supply possible policies; the proposed combined threshold remains to be validated in a pilot.</data>
  <data key="d52">engineering proposal</data>
  <data key="d53">Revised editorial plan 2026-10-09</data>
  <data key="d54">LEGACY_EDITORIAL_GRAPH</data>
  <data key="d55">2026-10-09 corrected editorial crosswalk</data>
</node>
