<node id="O001">
  <data key="d31">The Code Nobody Reads</data>
  <data key="d32">practitioner_article</data>
  <data key="d54">CURATED_PRACTITIONER_NOT_RESEARCH</data>
  <data key="d33">Verification &amp; review</data>
  <data key="d34">2026</data>
  <data key="d35">Addy Osmani</data>
  <data key="d36">https://addyo.substack.com/p/the-code-nobody-reads</data>
  <data key="d126">https://addyo.substack.com/p/the-code-nobody-reads</data>
  <data key="d184">https://addyo.substack.com/p/the-code-nobody-reads</data>
  <data key="d131">2026-09-28</data>
  <data key="d37">When human line-by-line reading no longer scales, independently justified checks and accountable approval must replace its assurance role.</data>
  <data key="d38">Predictions and vendor/company statistics are reported second-hand; do not treat a risk-tier policy as externally validated.</data>
  <data key="d39">Use explicit risk tiers and an evidence dossier rather than assuming every line is read; Keep independent tests and a named human acceptance authority; Protect mentoring, explainability and reviewer capacity.</data>
  <data key="d40">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d44">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d43">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d41">A</data>
  <data key="d45">article webpage read; linked empirical sources not all independently audited</data>
  <data key="d51">Osmani revises his earlier advice to inspect every generated line: routine low-risk changes may receive automated first-pass review, while high-blast-radius changes retain deeper scrutiny. He argues that review also transmitted knowledge and that engineering teams must retain both understanding and authority to refuse a release.</data>
  <data key="d222">When human line-by-line reading no longer scales, independently justified checks and accountable approval must replace its assurance role.</data>
  <data key="d223">Use explicit risk tiers and an evidence dossier rather than assuming every line is read; Keep independent tests and a named human acceptance authority; Protect mentoring, explainability and reviewer capacity.</data>
  <data key="d42">2026</data>
  <data key="d50">8.8</data>
  <data key="d224">C08</data>
  <data key="d225">Direct</data>
  <data key="d52">curated practitioner article (not a research study)</data>
  <data key="d226">false</data>
  <data key="d227">W093;W100;W111</data>
  <data key="d53">Osmani public Substack sitemaps 2024/2025/2026 plus individual source URLs; independent editorial review 2026-10-10</data>
  <data key="d55">Overlay only; does not change any original W001-W193 evidence classification</data>
  <data key="d228">The page includes a Teleport-sponsored passage, alongside quoted vendor/self-reported statistics. Its risk-calibrated review argument is practitioner commentary, not a controlled intervention; validate independent primary metrics before reusing any numbers.</data>
  <data key="d229">yes</data>
  <data key="d48">https://addyo.substack.com/sitemap/2026</data>
  <data key="d49">Substack essay, not a peer-reviewed research paper</data>
  <data key="d230">REVISED</data>
  <data key="d231">true</data>
  <data key="d232">2026-10-10</data>
  <data key="d233">comparison/analogy, not observed citation, causal validation or replication</data>
  <data key="d234">The page includes a Teleport-sponsored passage, alongside quoted vendor/self-reported statistics. Its risk-calibrated review argument is practitioner commentary, not a controlled intervention; validate independent primary metrics before reusing any numbers.</data>
  <data key="d235">Secondhand empirical or vendor statistics must not be mistaken for independently replicated studies.; Disclose the explicitly marked sponsorship and vendor telemetry limitations in the September 2026 article.</data>
  <data key="d236">AOS001</data>
  <data key="d237">O001</data>
