<node id="O002">
  <data key="d31">Brownfield Agentic Engineering</data>
  <data key="d32">practitioner_article</data>
  <data key="d54">CURATED_PRACTITIONER_NOT_RESEARCH</data>
  <data key="d33">System knowledge &amp; memory</data>
  <data key="d34">2026</data>
  <data key="d35">Addy Osmani</data>
  <data key="d36">https://addyo.substack.com/p/brownfield-agentic-engineering</data>
  <data key="d126">https://addyo.substack.com/p/brownfield-agentic-engineering</data>
  <data key="d184">https://addyo.substack.com/p/brownfield-agentic-engineering</data>
  <data key="d131">2026-09-14</data>
  <data key="d37">Reliable agents in mature codebases need recovered tacit constraints and regression evidence before modifying code.</data>
  <data key="d38">Practitioner interpretation; not primary experimental evidence.</data>
  <data key="d39">Inventory external invariants and dependencies before delegation; Make brownfield changes incremental and reversible; Verify old behavior alongside new acceptance criteria.</data>
  <data key="d40">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d44">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d43">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d41">A</data>
  <data key="d45">article webpage read; linked empirical sources not all independently audited</data>
  <data key="d51">The essay stresses that a repository rarely captures the full behavior of a legacy service: contracts, migrations, user journeys and institutional knowledge remain external. Small reversible changes, context gathering and reproducible tests are offered as controls against locally successful but architecturally wrong agent patches.</data>
  <data key="d222">Reliable agents in mature codebases need recovered tacit constraints and regression evidence before modifying code.</data>
  <data key="d223">Inventory external invariants and dependencies before delegation; Make brownfield changes incremental and reversible; Verify old behavior alongside new acceptance criteria.</data>
