<node id="O007">
  <data key="d31">Agentic Code Quality</data>
  <data key="d32">practitioner_article</data>
  <data key="d54">CURATED_PRACTITIONER_NOT_RESEARCH</data>
  <data key="d33">Code quality &amp; security</data>
  <data key="d34">2026</data>
  <data key="d35">Addy Osmani</data>
  <data key="d36">https://addyo.substack.com/p/agentic-code-quality</data>
  <data key="d126">https://addyo.substack.com/p/agentic-code-quality</data>
  <data key="d184">https://addyo.substack.com/p/agentic-code-quality</data>
  <data key="d131">2026-08-08</data>
  <data key="d37">Quality assurance for generated code increasingly depends on repeatable harness checks, not only manual inspection or agent opinion.</data>
  <data key="d38">Practitioner interpretation; not primary experimental evidence.</data>
  <data key="d39">Move quality requirements into repeatable repository checks and agent harness gates; Track defects, architectural fit and post-merge maintenance—not merely model review approval; Retain risk-sensitive human review because multiple models can share error modes.</data>
  <data key="d40">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d44">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d43">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d41">A</data>
  <data key="d45">article webpage read; linked empirical sources not all independently audited</data>
  <data key="d51">Osmani notes the scalability limit of inspecting high-volume agent diffs and the instability of repeated model-review judgments. He favors consistent repository rules, static checks, tests and explicit quality gates surrounding generation, while keeping focused human scrutiny for risky changes.</data>
  <data key="d222">Quality assurance for generated code increasingly depends on repeatable harness checks, not only manual inspection or agent opinion.</data>
  <data key="d223">Move quality requirements into repeatable repository checks and agent harness gates; Track defects, architectural fit and post-merge maintenance—not merely model review approval; Retain risk-sensitive human review because multiple models can share error modes.</data>
