<node id="O010">
  <data key="d31">Agentic Autonomy Levels</data>
  <data key="d32">practitioner_article</data>
  <data key="d54">CURATED_PRACTITIONER_NOT_RESEARCH</data>
  <data key="d33">What agents can do</data>
  <data key="d34">2026</data>
  <data key="d35">Addy Osmani</data>
  <data key="d36">https://addyo.substack.com/p/agentic-autonomy-levels</data>
  <data key="d126">https://addyo.substack.com/p/agentic-autonomy-levels</data>
  <data key="d184">https://addyo.substack.com/p/agentic-autonomy-levels</data>
  <data key="d131">2026-07-03</data>
  <data key="d37">Osmani separates autonomy into agency and orchestration axes, describing six operating levels from suggestions to management-by-exception.</data>
  <data key="d38">Practitioner interpretation; not primary experimental evidence.</data>
  <data key="d39">Grade individual-agent initiative separately from multi-agent orchestration; Use the six levels as a contextual operational taxonomy, not a validated maturity index; Set an explicit risk and verification ceiling for delegated work.</data>
  <data key="d40">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d44">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d43">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d41">A</data>
  <data key="d45">article webpage read; linked empirical sources not all independently audited</data>
  <data key="d51">Osmani argues that a one-dimensional autonomy ladder hides the difference between what one agent may do and the ability to coordinate many agents. He proposes two axes—agency and orchestration—and six practical levels: assist, supervised action, scoped task delegation, goal-driven autonomy, parallel delegation, and managed-by-exception orchestration. Risk, reversibility, independent checks and escalation limit the appropriate level for any task.</data>
  <data key="d222">Osmani separates autonomy into agency and orchestration axes, describing six operating levels from suggestions to management-by-exception.</data>
  <data key="d223">Grade individual-agent initiative separately from multi-agent orchestration; Use the six levels as a contextual operational taxonomy, not a validated maturity index; Set an explicit risk and verification ceiling for delegated work.</data>
  <data key="d42">2026</data>
  <data key="d50">3.3</data>
