<node id="O012">
  <data key="d31">Loop Engineering</data>
  <data key="d32">practitioner_article</data>
  <data key="d54">CURATED_PRACTITIONER_NOT_RESEARCH</data>
  <data key="d33">Engineering structure</data>
  <data key="d34">2026</data>
  <data key="d35">Addy Osmani</data>
  <data key="d36">https://addyo.substack.com/p/loop-engineering</data>
  <data key="d126">https://addyo.substack.com/p/loop-engineering</data>
  <data key="d184">https://addyo.substack.com/p/loop-engineering</data>
  <data key="d131">2026-06-08</data>
  <data key="d37">Osmani identifies five interoperable loop-building primitives—scheduling, worktrees, skills, connectors and subagents—plus durable external memory.</data>
  <data key="d38">A practitioner engineering pattern; the article does not establish comparative loop reliability by controlled experiment.</data>
  <data key="d39">Schedule discovery and triage but bound side effects; Isolate parallel work in worktrees; share intentions via tested skills/connectors and durable state; Separate the maker from the checker, measure token usage, and escalate when independent checks fail.</data>
  <data key="d40">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d44">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d43">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d41">A</data>
  <data key="d45">article webpage read; linked empirical sources not all independently audited</data>
  <data key="d51">Loop engineering means configuring a system that discovers work, dispatches agents, checks outputs, records progress and repeats rather than manually prompting each step. Osmani identifies five recurring primitives: automations/scheduling, isolated Git worktrees, reusable skills, plugins and connectors, and specialized subagents. A separate persistent memory or task board is the sixth requirement for continuity. He also warns about verification, comprehension debt and unpredictable token costs.</data>
  <data key="d222">Osmani identifies five interoperable loop-building primitives—scheduling, worktrees, skills, connectors and subagents—plus durable external memory.</data>
  <data key="d223">Schedule discovery and triage but bound side effects; Isolate parallel work in worktrees; share intentions via tested skills/connectors and durable state; Separate the maker from the checker, measure token usage, and escalate when independent checks fail.</data>
  <data key="d42">2026</data>
  <data key="d50">3.6</data>
  <data key="d224">C03</data>
  <data key="d225">Direct</data>
  <data key="d52">curated practitioner article (not a research study)</data>
  <data key="d226">false</data>
  <data key="d227">W075;W095;W191</data>
  <data key="d53">Osmani public Substack sitemaps 2024/2025/2026 plus individual source URLs; independent editorial review 2026-10-10</data>
  <data key="d55">Overlay only; does not change any original W001-W193 evidence classification</data>
  <data key="d228">A practitioner engineering pattern; the article does not establish comparative loop reliability by controlled experiment. Framework or market hypothesis is an authored practitioner proposal, not an empirically validated scale or intervention.</data>
  <data key="d229">yes</data>
  <data key="d48">https://addyo.substack.com/sitemap/2026</data>
