  <data key="d126">https://addyo.substack.com/p/death-of-the-ide</data>
  <data key="d184">https://addyo.substack.com/p/death-of-the-ide</data>
  <data key="d131">2026-03-20</data>
  <data key="d37">Asynchronous agent orchestration may become the primary developer control surface, while editors remain tools for targeted inspection.</data>
  <data key="d38">Practitioner interpretation; not primary experimental evidence.</data>
  <data key="d39">Teach delegation and inspection as separate activities; Compare orchestration tools on observable state and control; Treat claims about the IDE dying as forecasts.</data>
  <data key="d40">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d44">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d43">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d41">B</data>
  <data key="d45">article webpage read; linked empirical sources not all independently audited</data>
  <data key="d51">Osmani discusses workbench and control-plane interfaces for coordinating cloud and local coding agents. The claim is about a shift in where engineers plan, inspect and approve work, not proof that code editors will disappear.</data>
  <data key="d222">Asynchronous agent orchestration may become the primary developer control surface, while editors remain tools for targeted inspection.</data>
  <data key="d223">Teach delegation and inspection as separate activities; Compare orchestration tools on observable state and control; Treat claims about the IDE dying as forecasts.</data>
