  <data key="d131">2026-02-12</data>
  <data key="d37">Long-lived engineering teams depend on trust, clear decision-making, effective communication and organizational stewardship.</data>
  <data key="d38">Personal career retrospective; no original AI-specific field experiment.</data>
  <data key="d39">Make decision rights and handoffs visible; Value team reliability above heroic individual throughput; Use as background principles, not an AI impact study.</data>
  <data key="d40">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d44">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d43">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d41">B</data>
  <data key="d45">article webpage read; linked empirical sources not all independently audited</data>
  <data key="d51">A second retrospective about long-term team effectiveness stresses the structures around projects: communication, ownership, priorities, collaboration and shared decisions. These lessons provide organizational context rather than new research on AI agents.</data>
  <data key="d222">Long-lived engineering teams depend on trust, clear decision-making, effective communication and organizational stewardship.</data>
  <data key="d223">Make decision rights and handoffs visible; Value team reliability above heroic individual throughput; Use as background principles, not an AI impact study.</data>
