<node id="O017">
  <data key="d31">The 80% Problem in Agentic Coding</data>
  <data key="d32">practitioner_article</data>
  <data key="d54">CURATED_PRACTITIONER_NOT_RESEARCH</data>
  <data key="d33">Skills &amp; learning</data>
  <data key="d34">2026</data>
  <data key="d35">Addy Osmani</data>
  <data key="d36">https://addyo.substack.com/p/the-80-problem-in-agentic-coding</data>
  <data key="d126">https://addyo.substack.com/p/the-80-problem-in-agentic-coding</data>
  <data key="d184">https://addyo.substack.com/p/the-80-problem-in-agentic-coding</data>
  <data key="d131">2026-01-28</data>
  <data key="d37">When agents implement most code, developers can accumulate comprehension debt even while shipping more changes.</data>
  <data key="d38">The 80% figure expresses an illustrative workflow shift; it is not a population-level experimental estimate.</data>
  <data key="d39">Require explain-back, behavioral traces and repair exercises; Measure whether an engineer can modify agent-written code later; Treat the 80% phrase as illustration, not a measured universal ratio.</data>
  <data key="d40">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d44">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d43">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d41">A</data>
  <data key="d45">article webpage read; linked empirical sources not all independently audited</data>
  <data key="d51">Extending his earlier "70% problem", Osmani frames agent-heavy development as an inversion between authorship and understanding. Speed moves the bottleneck toward explaining decisions, tracing behavior and maintaining the code after the generation session ends.</data>
  <data key="d222">When agents implement most code, developers can accumulate comprehension debt even while shipping more changes.</data>
  <data key="d223">Require explain-back, behavioral traces and repair exercises; Measure whether an engineer can modify agent-written code later; Treat the 80% phrase as illustration, not a measured universal ratio.</data>
  <data key="d42">2026</data>
  <data key="d50">10.8</data>
