  <data key="d131">2025-12-29</data>
  <data key="d37">React-related AI success is highly task-dependent, with integration and usability much harder to establish than component scaffolding.</data>
  <data key="d38">Numerical benchmark claims are a synthesis of separate benchmarks with different tasks, versions and measurement methods; not directly comparable.</data>
  <data key="d39">Benchmark scores have task- and version-specific denominators; Use browser tests and accessibility review for end-to-end UI changes; Treat reported percentages as third-party snapshot results, not the author’s experimental measurement.</data>
  <data key="d40">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d44">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d43">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d41">B</data>
  <data key="d45">article webpage read; linked empirical sources not all independently audited</data>
  <data key="d51">Using third-party web and coding benchmarks, Osmani contrasts relatively tractable isolated UI tasks with weaker multi-step integration. He recommends context-rich specifications, UI standards, accessibility checks and browser-level evaluation instead of equating code generation with product quality.</data>
  <data key="d222">React-related AI success is highly task-dependent, with integration and usability much harder to establish than component scaffolding.</data>
  <data key="d223">Benchmark scores have task- and version-specific denominators; Use browser tests and accessibility review for end-to-end UI changes; Treat reported percentages as third-party snapshot results, not the author’s experimental measurement.</data>
