  <data key="d229">yes</data>
  <data key="d48">https://addyo.substack.com/sitemap/2025</data>
  <data key="d49">Substack essay, not a peer-reviewed research paper</data>
  <data key="d230">REVISED</data>
  <data key="d231">true</data>
  <data key="d232">2026-10-10</data>
  <data key="d233">comparison/analogy, not observed citation, causal validation or replication</data>
  <data key="d234">Forward-looking economic scenario; no primary adoption or welfare evaluation. Framework or market hypothesis is an authored practitioner proposal, not an empirically validated scale or intervention.</data>
  <data key="d235">The central proposition is a distributional/demand shift toward personally created software, not choosing an autonomy level by least cost.; Firm-level AI hiring studies do not investigate the novel long-tail personal-software demand hypothesized by the article; retain the lifecycle ROI paper only as economic contrast.; State the original essay’s demand-creation thesis explicitly.; Emphasize personalization and software demand rather than autonomy-cascade cost optimization.; The paper is a qualitative demand scenario; employment comparison is contextual rather than observed outcome.; Do not promote conceptual taxonomy or market hypotheses into established research outcomes.</data>
