  <data key="d184">https://addyo.substack.com/p/user-centric-engineering</data>
  <data key="d131">2024-11-15</data>
  <data key="d37">Customer needs and observable outcomes—not lines of code—should determine whether an engineering change succeeds.</data>
  <data key="d38">General user-experience and product principles, no original AI evaluation.</data>
  <data key="d39">Start with a concrete user problem and a user-observable success criterion; Validate design trade-offs through user interviews, usage data, and support feedback; Use only as background product engineering context, not as evidence of AI productivity.</data>
  <data key="d40">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d44">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d43">Practitioner essay / technical guidance (not peer-reviewed)</data>
  <data key="d41">B</data>
  <data key="d45">article webpage read; linked empirical sources not all independently audited</data>
  <data key="d51">Osmani argues that engineering decisions should start with user needs and observable benefit rather than technical elegance. He offers questions to ask before development, during iterative implementation and after launch, supported by practices such as support-ticket analysis, behavioral observation, interviews and product feedback. The article does not evaluate AI coding systems.</data>
  <data key="d222">Customer needs and observable outcomes—not lines of code—should determine whether an engineering change succeeds.</data>
  <data key="d223">Start with a concrete user problem and a user-observable success criterion; Validate design trade-offs through user interviews, usage data, and support feedback; Use only as background product engineering context, not as evidence of AI productivity.</data>
