  <data key="d37">Examines Indeed/FRED vacancy indices across countries and discusses competing explanations for the decline, including the post-pandemic correction and AI expectations.</data>
  <data key="d222">Examines Indeed/FRED vacancy indices across countries and discusses competing explanations for the decline, including the post-pandemic correction and AI expectations.</data>
  <data key="d51">Examines Indeed/FRED vacancy indices across countries and discusses competing explanations for the decline, including the post-pandemic correction and AI expectations.</data>
  <data key="d223">Job-posting counts are not employment counts. Cross-country similarities weaken a single-policy explanation and do not identify AI as the cause.</data>
  <data key="d262">Visible arguments, author-reported observations and explicitly labeled outlines; no reconstruction of hidden sections.</data>
  <data key="d38">Updated 5 May 2025. Legal/tax timing assertions require separate verification before reuse. This non-Pulse-titled blog article is an excerpt of a Pulse issue originally published 2025-02-13. It passes the literal title-prefix rule; it is excluded by the optional stricter Pulse-origin filter. The parent issue is provenance only, not another P article. Public availability does not imply an independent check of every paragraph or chart. No microdata replication, controlled product test, or independent-rater agreement study was performed.</data>
  <data key="d39">Vacancy trends frame competing labor-market explanations; they cannot alone establish employment loss caused by AI.</data>
