  <data key="d37">Explains MCP using a database-tool example and an interview with a protocol co-creator.</data>
  <data key="d222">Explains MCP using a database-tool example and an interview with a protocol co-creator.</data>
  <data key="d51">Explains MCP using a database-tool example and an interview with a protocol co-creator. Connects client-server architecture and IDE extensibility to credential-access risks.</data>
  <data key="d223">The public demonstration illustrates improved access to context and tools while warning that capability expansion does not provide a security boundary.</data>
  <data key="d262">Visible arguments, author-reported observations and explicitly labeled outlines; no reconstruction of hidden sections.</data>
  <data key="d38">Protocol and implementation descriptions are dated April 2025, not a security assessment of every later MCP implementation. Subscriber-only article remainder not reviewed. No microdata replication, controlled product test, or independent-rater agreement study was performed.</data>
  <data key="d39">The database/tool example explains how coding assistants acquire context and capabilities through MCP. Security and authorization remain separate design duties; this is not an empirical security evaluation.</data>
