  <data key="d51">Investigates claims that Builder.ai was merely a human-operated substitute for AI. Former-employee accounts describe a code-generation product alongside human fulfilment; a hypothetical human-operated design is an explanatory contrast, not a verified internal architecture.</data>
  <data key="d223">The account challenges a simplified corporate narrative. It does not independently verify the product’s reliability, staffing, financial history or productivity.</data>
  <data key="d262">Visible arguments, author-reported observations and explicitly labeled outlines; no reconstruction of hidden sections.</data>
  <data key="d38">Claims about internal operations are attributed to former staff; corporate-failure explanations remain interpretations. This non-Pulse-titled blog article is an excerpt of a Pulse issue originally published 2025-06-12. It passes the literal title-prefix rule; it is excluded by the optional stricter Pulse-origin filter. The parent issue is provenance only, not another P article. Public availability does not imply an independent check of every paragraph or chart. No microdata replication, controlled product test, or independent-rater agreement study was performed.</data>
  <data key="d39">Industry fact-checking distinguishes reported AI code generation from human fulfilment. Neither a hypothetical architecture nor employee accounts validate a capability benchmark.</data>
