  <data key="d37">Discusses METR’s experienced-maintainer experiment and possible workflow explanations.</data>
  <data key="d222">Discusses METR’s experienced-maintainer experiment and possible workflow explanations.</data>
  <data key="d51">Discusses METR’s experienced-maintainer experiment and possible workflow explanations. A targeted primary-source check confirms 16 developers and 246 issues randomized by issue to AI allowed or disallowed, not separate developer treatment groups.</data>
  <data key="d223">The primary study reports slower completion in this specific early-2025 setting. P052’s broader all-tools inference and proposed familiarity explanation remain interpretations; neither is independent causal validation.</data>
  <data key="d262">Visible arguments, author-reported observations and explicitly labeled outlines; no reconstruction of hidden sections.</data>
  <data key="d38">P052 says 136 issues and implies assignment by developer. METR’s primary methodology reports 246 issues and issue-level randomization, with developers working in both conditions. Do not reproduce the secondary account’s conflicting design description. A primary abstract/methodology check was performed, not a full-paper audit or statistical replication. This is a retitled Pulse excerpt; preserve its existing series provenance. Public availability does not imply an independent check of every paragraph or chart. No microdata replication, controlled product test, or independent-rater agreement study was performed.</data>
  <data key="d39">This is secondary commentary on W023, not a replication; the all-tools extrapolation and causal learning-curve explanation are unsupported.</data>
  <data key="d40">Industry/practitioner publication — secondary research commentary</data>
  <data key="d44">Industry/practitioner publication — secondary research commentary</data>
