  <data key="d256"></data>
  <data key="d257">true</data>
  <data key="d258">newsletter</data>
  <data key="d259">Deepdives</data>
  <data key="d260"></data>
  <data key="d261">[]</data>
  <data key="d37">A survey of newsletter readers examines coding-tool adoption, model preferences, seniority and organizational differences.</data>
  <data key="d222">A survey of newsletter readers examines coding-tool adoption, model preferences, seniority and organizational differences.</data>
  <data key="d51">A survey of newsletter readers examines coding-tool adoption, model preferences, seniority and organizational differences.</data>
  <data key="d223">The volunteer survey reports associations among tool use, role, company size and sentiment. Procurement explanations are author hypotheses, not identified causes; neither population market share nor causal productivity follows from these responses.</data>
  <data key="d262">Visible arguments, author-reported observations and explicitly labeled outlines; no reconstruction of hidden sections.</data>
  <data key="d38">Survey ran January 27–February 17, 2026; same cohort as the two impact follow-ups. The author reports 906 responses for the shared 2026 cohort. This is not an independently counted sample; comparisons with other survey waves are not a matched longitudinal panel. The same page calls the paid supplement 65 pages near the beginning and 35 pages near the end. It also pairs 55% regular agent usage with 507 people; 507/906 is about 56%, so the response denominator or rounding needs clarification. Neither discrepancy is silently corrected. Public availability does not imply an independent check of every paragraph or chart. No microdata replication, controlled product test, or independent-rater agreement study was performed.</data>
  <data key="d39">The reader survey measures reported usage and sentiment, not causal productivity or population-wide adoption.</data>
  <data key="d40">Industry/practitioner publication — reader survey analysis</data>
  <data key="d44">Industry/practitioner publication — reader survey analysis</data>
  <data key="d263">public_article_paid_extras</data>
  <data key="d264">Public main article available; separate paid supplementary material not reviewed</data>
  <data key="d265">REVIEWED_PUBLIC_PASSAGE_CLASSIFICATION</data>
  <data key="d266">The reader survey measures reported usage and sentiment, not causal productivity or population-wide adoption.</data>
