  <data key="d37">An industry case describes Uber’s AI development platform, shared context, internal agents and review-related tooling.</data>
  <data key="d222">An industry case describes Uber’s AI development platform, shared context, internal agents and review-related tooling.</data>
  <data key="d51">An industry case describes Uber’s AI development platform, shared context, internal agents and review-related tooling.</data>
  <data key="d223">The report describes internal tools, organizational context and patterns of agent adoption at Uber. These accounts suggest engineering dependencies worth testing elsewhere, but do not isolate the causal effect of platform integration on delivery outcomes.</data>
  <data key="d262">Visible arguments, author-reported observations and explicitly labeled outlines; no reconstruction of hidden sections.</data>
  <data key="d38">Original publication March 10; updated March 11. Reported adoption percentages use different denominators. Author discloses former employment at Uber. The source’s correction distinguishes 84% agentic-tool adoption from 65–72% IDE code-generation figures and CLI-specific measures; those denominators must not be interchanged. An author correction in the March 12, 2026 Pulse distinguishes the IDE-generated-code share from an earlier erroneous percentage. Original date, updated text and company-level denominators remain separate. Subscriber-only article remainder not reviewed. No microdata replication, controlled product test, or independent-rater agreement study was performed.</data>
  <data key="d39">Uber's internal integration and company context are operational conditions that limit transfer of reported results.</data>
  <data key="d40">Industry/practitioner publication — reported company case</data>
