  <data key="d51">Orosz contrasts Meta’s earlier engineering culture with reported AI-related restructuring, pressure on developers and weakening operational safeguards. The piece combines historical comparison and contemporary employee accounts.</data>
  <data key="d223">The analysis raises a governance failure hypothesis: adoption pressure can undermine ownership and assurance. Its allegations and causal interpretation should remain attributed to the reporting.</data>
  <data key="d262">Visible arguments, author-reported observations and explicitly labeled outlines; no reconstruction of hidden sections.</data>
  <data key="d38">Claims about management intent and organizational consequences are attributed reporting; the article is not a controlled evaluation of AI or a verified causal explanation. Public availability does not imply an independent check of every paragraph or chart. No microdata replication, controlled product test, or independent-rater agreement study was performed.</data>
  <data key="d39">The report raises an organizational-risk hypothesis; employee allegations and editorial causal interpretations remain attributed.</data>
