  <data key="d37">An interview with Simon Eskildsen connects back-of-the-envelope resource estimates, long-term systems experience and the design of turbopuffer.</data>
  <data key="d222">An interview with Simon Eskildsen connects back-of-the-envelope resource estimates, long-term systems experience and the design of turbopuffer.</data>
  <data key="d51">An interview with Simon Eskildsen connects back-of-the-envelope resource estimates, long-term systems experience and the design of turbopuffer. Performance assumptions are checked against hardware limits and observed behavior.</data>
  <data key="d223">A gap between expected and measured performance can expose a bad benchmark or a bad model. Reported startup cost savings remain a case study, not an AI-productivity experiment.</data>
  <data key="d262">Visible arguments, author-reported observations and explicitly labeled outlines; no reconstruction of hidden sections.</data>
  <data key="d38">The publication discloses that turbopuffer sponsors its podcast and states that this deepdive is editorially independent. Public availability does not imply an independent check of every paragraph or chart. No microdata replication, controlled product test, or independent-rater agreement study was performed.</data>
  <data key="d39">Resource estimates checked against observation illustrate model calibration; startup savings are not coding-agent ROI evidence.</data>
