  <data key="d37">Interviews with engineering leaders examine career breaks, founder expectations, cost pressure, changing team sizes and renewed interest in hands-on work.</data>
  <data key="d222">Interviews with engineering leaders examine career breaks, founder expectations, cost pressure, changing team sizes and renewed interest in hands-on work.</data>
  <data key="d51">Interviews with engineering leaders examine career breaks, founder expectations, cost pressure, changing team sizes and renewed interest in hands-on work. The preview highlights tension between rapid prototyping and operational ownership.</data>
  <data key="d223">The interviews identify plausible mechanisms and experiences, not the prevalence of leadership departures or a causal effect of AI on employment.</data>
  <data key="d262">Visible arguments, author-reported observations and explicitly labeled outlines; no reconstruction of hidden sections.</data>
  <data key="d38">Selected career-break interviews and the six-of-ten subgroup do not estimate a population departure rate. Subscriber-only article remainder not reviewed. No microdata replication, controlled product test, or independent-rater agreement study was performed.</data>
  <data key="d39">Leadership-break interviews concern ownership, incentives and transitions, without measuring the prevalence of AI-caused exits.</data>
